# FIXED (commit 3bbc010): an axiom whose kername collided with Lean's Nat.add.
#
# The program applies the axiom `_Nat.add` (module literally named `_Nat`) to
# 6 and 7; its Peregrine attributes (prog.attr) realize the axiom as int63
# multiplication, so OCaml prints 42.
#
# Before the fix the Java axiom table was keyed by mangled strings, `_Nat.add`
# mangled like `Nat.add`, and Java SILENTLY printed 6 + 7 = 13. Now the table is
# keyed by kernames, `_Nat.add` is unknown, and Java throws
# "axiom ... not implemented": the intended loud failure, since the Java
# backend does not read attribute files. Hence `xfail` for java. `xfail` never
# absorbs a wrong value, so a regression to 13 would fail the run. Source:
# lambox-to-java-examples/src/ExampleAxiomCollision.ard. No `c` backend:
# `peregrine c` refuses unrealized axioms.
expected=42
backends=java ocaml
runtime=int63
xfail=Java ignores Peregrine attribute remaps, so the unknown axiom _Nat.add throws "not implemented" (intended; before 3bbc010 it silently printed 13)
note=_Nat.add 6 7 with _Nat.add remapped to int63 multiplication; must not resolve to Lean's Nat.add
